

The @Override Annotation
- Every Java class implicitly inherits from a master parent class called Object, which provides
 default (and often unhelpful) versions of these three methods. By using @Override, you tell the compiler 
 that you are intentionally replacing that parent behavior. If you accidentally misspell the method (e.g., hashcode() instead of hashCode()),
  the @Override annotation forces the compiler to break the build, protecting you from a silent bug.

Step 1: The hashCode() Method (The Storage)
- When you store or look up an object in a Map, Java calls hashCode() to calculate an integer value that dictates 
  exactly which memory "bucket" the object belongs in.

Objects.hash(merchantId, planTier): This utility reliably generates the same integer as long as the inputs are 
identical. If two objects are considered equal, they absolutely must route to the exact same hash code bucket.

Step 2: The equals() Method (The Final Verification)
- Because multiple distinct objects can occasionally generate the same hash code (a collision), the Map navigates
  to the assigned bucket and uses equals() to verify the actual data inside.

`if (this == o) return true;`:  If the variables point to the exact same object in memory, it skips the rest of the 
 checks and immediately returns true.

`if (o == null || getClass() != o.getClass()) return false;`: The safety check. This guarantees the incoming object is 
 not null and is specifically a MerchantAccount, preventing your backend from crashing with a NullPointerException or a class cast error.

`MerchantAccount that = (MerchantAccount) o;`: Once the safety checks pass, the generic Object o is cast into a MerchantAccount so its specific properties can be accessed.

`return Objects.equals(merchantId, that.merchantId) && ...`: The actual data comparison. Objects.equals() safely compares the fields without crashing if one of them happens to be null.